    lectures: [
      {
        script: "Good afternoon, everyone. Today I want to challenge a widely held assumption: the belief that students should specialize in a single field as early as possible to get a head start in their careers.\nIt is perfectly understandable why this idea appeals to families. The job market looks fiercely competitive, and early specialization appears efficient. Yet research on career outcomes tells a rather different story. A well-known study of engineers at a large technology firm found that the most innovative patent holders were not those with the narrowest training. They were engineers who had also worked in, or seriously studied, a second domain, such as biology or design. Their breakthroughs came from connecting ideas that specialists treated as unrelated.\nA broad education offers another advantage that is easy to overlook: adaptability. Industries now restructure themselves every decade or so. The specific software you master today may be obsolete in fifteen years, but the ability to learn a new field does not expire. Students who have practiced connecting disciplines tend to retrain far more confidently.\nLet me be clear: I am not arguing against expertise. Deep knowledge of one field remains essential. What I am criticizing is premature narrowness, the pressure to abandon everything else at eighteen. The strongest programs, in my view, combine a firm core major with genuine exposure to the humanities and social sciences. T-shaped professionals, as they are called, bring depth in one area and breadth across many, and those are precisely the people who tend to lead rather than simply follow.",
        analysis: {
          passage: "讲座先树靶（早专业越早越好）再反驳：用工程师专利研究证明跨领域者更具创新力，再论适应性，最后提出 T 型人才主张。转折信号 Yet、Let me be clear 是观点题定位点。",
          sentence: "否定转移与表语从句并用：The strongest programs ... combine a firm core major with genuine exposure to the humanities；the people who tend to lead rather than simply follow（rather than 取舍）。",
          vocabulary: "specialize /ˈspeʃəlaɪz/ v. 专门研究<br>patent /ˈpætnt/ n. 专利<br>domain /dəˈmeɪn/ n. 领域<br>adaptability /əˌdæptəˈbɪləti/ n. 适应力<br>obsolete /ˌɒbsəˈliːt/ adj. 过时的<br>premature /ˌpreməˈtʃʊər/ adj. 过早的"
        },
        questions: [
          { q: "What assumption does the professor intend to challenge?", options: ["That engineers earn more than designers", "That the job market has become less competitive", "That students should specialize in one field early", "That humanities students cannot find jobs"], answer: "C", tip: "首句即点明 challenge ... the belief that students should specialize ... as early as possible。" },
          { q: "What did the study of engineers find?", options: ["Narrow training produced the best patents", "Biology majors made poor engineers", "Design knowledge replaced technical knowledge", "The most innovative engineers had cross-field experience"], answer: "D", tip: "最具创新的专利持有者是 also worked in or studied a second domain 的工程师。" },
          { q: "According to the professor, what is the main advantage of broad training?", options: ["It develops adaptability when industries change", "It lets students graduate much earlier", "It guarantees a higher starting salary", "It reduces the cost of university education"], answer: "A", tip: "A broad education offers another advantage ... adaptability；行业约十年重构一次。" }
        ]
      },
      {
        script: "Thank you all for attending this mandatory session on academic integrity. I know the topic sounds dry, but please listen carefully, because the rules changed this year.\nLast year, five percent of our undergraduates were found responsible for some form of academic dishonesty. That figure includes the obvious offenses, such as copying exam answers or bringing in forbidden notes, but most cases were less dramatic. They involved improper collaboration on homework, fabricated data in lab reports, or failure to put quotation marks around sentences copied from websites.\nThe biggest change concerns artificial intelligence. Using an AI tool to brainstorm or to check your grammar is permitted. Submitting AI-generated paragraphs as your own writing is considered plagiarism, exactly as if you had copied them from another student. In addition, in any exam labeled AI-prohibited, even opening a chatbot on your phone is treated as cheating, regardless of whether you actually use the answer.\nWhat are the consequences? A first offense in a minor assignment typically results in a zero for that assignment and a required workshop. A second offense, or any offense during a final examination, is referred to the disciplinary board and can lead to suspension. My advice is simple: when you are unsure whether something is allowed, ask your instructor first, keep your drafts, and give credit to every source you use. Honest work is never something you have to hide.",
        analysis: {
          passage: "校纪宣讲：违规比例与常见情形、AI 使用新规、两级处罚、四条避嫌建议。数字（5%）与规则对比（permitted vs plagiarism/prohibited）是命题重点。",
          sentence: "让步 + regardless of 表无条件：even opening a chatbot ... is treated as cheating, regardless of whether you actually use the answer（无论是否采用答案都算作弊）。",
          vocabulary: "integrity /ɪnˈteɡrəti/ n. 诚信<br>dishonesty /dɪsˈɒnɪsti/ n. 不诚实<br>collaboration /kəˌlæbəˈreɪʃn/ n. 合作<br>fabricate /ˈfæbrɪkeɪt/ v. 捏造<br>plagiarism /ˈpleɪdʒərɪzəm/ n. 剽窃<br>suspension /səˈspenʃn/ n. 停学"
        },
        questions: [
          { q: "What does the speaker say about last year's dishonesty cases?", options: ["Most involved copying during final exams", "Many were less obvious offenses like improper teamwork", "Five percent of graduates were expelled", "AI tools caused every single case"], answer: "B", tip: "most cases were less dramatic，涉及不当协作、捏造数据、漏标引用。" },
          { q: "What is the new rule on artificial intelligence?", options: ["AI may never be used in any assignment", "Only grammar checking with AI is punished", "Submitting AI-generated text counts as plagiarism", "Chatbots are allowed in all examinations"], answer: "C", tip: "Submitting AI-generated paragraphs as your own writing is considered plagiarism。" },
          { q: "What may happen after a second offense?", options: ["A verbal warning from the instructor", "A small fine paid to the library", "Automatic graduation with honors", "Referral to the disciplinary board and possible suspension"], answer: "D", tip: "第二次或在期末考试中违规 is referred to the disciplinary board and can lead to suspension。" }
        ]
      },
      {
        script: "Welcome back to Psychology of Learning. Today we will look at what decades of experiments actually tell us about effective studying, and some of the findings contradict what most students do.\nThe most popular study method on campus is probably rereading notes and highlighting textbooks. It feels productive, because the material becomes familiar. Unfortunately, dozens of studies show that rereading produces surprisingly weak long-term memory. Familiarity, it turns out, is easily mistaken for genuine knowledge.\nA far stronger technique is called retrieval practice, which simply means closing the book and trying to recall the material from memory. In a classic experiment, students who read a passage once and then spent the remaining time self-testing remembered fifty percent more a week later than students who used all the time to reread.\nThe second principle is spacing. Study the same topic on Monday, Wednesday, and Friday for thirty minutes each, rather than for ninety minutes on Thursday night. Each session partially fades, and the effort of reconstructing the memory strengthens it. This is why cramming can pass tomorrow's quiz but fails you at the final exam two months later.\nThe third principle concerns mixing topics. Interleaving, or alternating different problem types, feels harder and slower, yet it dramatically improves the ability to choose the right method on a novel question. So if you want to study smarter, test yourself often, spread the sessions out, and mix your practice. The methods that feel easiest are rarely the ones that teach the most.",
        analysis: {
          passage: "讲座介绍三大高效学习法：检索练习优于重读、间隔优于突击、交错练习优于单一重复。结构为'破除错觉—逐条给出原则与实验—总结'，实验数字与对比是高频考点。",
          sentence: "插入语与形式主语句：Familiarity, it turns out, is easily mistaken for genuine knowledge.（it turns out 作插入语）；结尾 The methods that feel easiest are rarely the ones that teach the most.（最高级 + rarely 含蓄否定）。",
          vocabulary: "contradict /ˌkɒntrəˈdɪkt/ v. 与……矛盾<br>retrieval /rɪˈtriːvl/ n. 检索；回忆<br>recall /rɪˈkɔːl/ v. 回忆<br>spacing /ˈspeɪsɪŋ/ n. 间隔（学习）<br>cram /kræm/ v. 突击死记<br>interleave /ˌɪntəˈliːv/ v. 交错进行"
        },
        questions: [
          { q: "Why do students tend to trust rereading?", options: ["It makes the material feel familiar and productive", "It has been proven to last for months", "It is recommended by every psychology study", "It takes the least physical effort"], answer: "A", tip: "It feels productive, because the material becomes familiar；熟悉感被误认为掌握。" },
          { q: "What happened in the classic retrieval-practice experiment?", options: ["Rereaders remembered fifty percent more", "Self-testers remembered half again as much a week later", "Both groups performed identically", "Crammers scored best on the final exam"], answer: "B", tip: "自测组一周后多记住 50%（remembered fifty percent more）。" },
          { q: "According to the spacing principle, why is cramming ineffective long-term?", options: ["Memories are never formed at all while cramming", "It only works for mathematics problems", "Material learned in one massed session fades quickly", "It forces students to skip their sleep entirely"], answer: "C", tip: "突击能应付明天小测，但两个月后期末考就失效，因为单次集中记忆消退快。" },
          { q: "What does interleaving refer to?", options: ["Reading notes aloud in a study group", "Studying one topic until it is mastered", "Highlighting passages in different colors", "Alternating between different types of problems"], answer: "D", tip: "Interleaving, or alternating different problem types，提升在新题中选择正确方法的能力。" }
        ]
      }
    ]
