        script: "If you have ever pulled an all-nighter before an exam and then struggled to remember what you studied, a recent psychology experiment helps explain why.\nScientists invited sixty college students to learn a list of paired words and short finger-tapping sequences, skills known to depend on different memory systems. Half the learners were then allowed a normal night of sleep, while the other half stayed awake in the laboratory under supervision. Two days later, everyone was tested. The sleep group recalled roughly twenty percent more word pairs and performed the tapping sequences both faster and more accurately, and brain recordings during their night showed distinctive bursts of activity associated with transferring memories into long-term storage.\nThe researchers were testing an older claim that sleep simply protects memories from new interference. Their results went further. Sleep did not merely freeze the learning; it appeared to reorganize it. Participants who slept were better at seeing hidden rules linking the word pairs, suggesting that memory consolidation supports insight rather than mere repetition.\nThe study also examined a ninety-minute nap. Students who napped after learning gained about half the benefit of a full night, enough to outperform peers who stayed awake. The authors caution that napping is a supplement, not a replacement, for regular sleep, and that sleeping well only after months of poor habits cannot repair accumulated deficits. For students, the practical message is familiar but now better supported: distributing study over several days and protecting nighttime sleep is more effective than any single marathon session, however heroic it feels.",
