        script: "Across much of the world, education policy is moving away from rigid age-based cohorts toward what is called competency-based education, an approach that advances students according to demonstrated mastery rather than time served. To understand its appeal, we must first examine the assumptions behind the traditional model.\nThe conventional system is elegant in administration: children born in the same year enter together, follow a fixed curriculum at a uniform pace, and move on after a set term, whether or not every student has fully understood the material. The design suited mass education in the industrial age, when systems needed to process large cohorts efficiently. Its weakness is equally clear. Students who miss an early concept, perhaps because of illness, a family move, or inadequate reading instruction, accumulate silent gaps that make later learning in mathematics or languages almost impossible, while students who already know the content sit bored through repeated lessons.\nCompetency-based models respond with three principles. First, mastery is explicit: standards describe precisely what a student should know and be able to do, and progression requires demonstrating that competence, often through projects or performance tasks rather than only multiple-choice tests. Second, time becomes flexible: some learners need more practice on fractions, others move quickly to advanced work, and both are treated as normal. Third, support is immediate; teachers use frequent short assessments to detect gaps within days rather than discovering them at an end-of-year examination.\nEvidence from early adopters is encouraging but conditional. Schools implementing the model faithfully, with strong coaching for teachers and redesigned transcripts that colleges understand, report stronger performance among struggling learners and higher engagement among advanced ones. However, superficial implementation creates new problems: labeling children as not ready can become a disguised form of tracking, and flexible pacing without adequate staffing simply leaves slow learners isolated.\nTechnology offers useful tools, from adaptive math platforms to digital portfolios, but it cannot define competence by itself, because deciding what students should master is a public and philosophical question. Competency-based education ultimately asks society a deeper question: is the purpose of schooling to sort children by date of birth, or to guarantee powerful learning to each one? Answering honestly requires far more than changing the schedule; it requires redesigning assessment, teaching, and our tolerance for unequal readiness.",
